1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,168
Turning a part into a machining plan takes a skilled machinist

2
00:00:05,168 --> 00:00:09,668
Software that plans it automatically is almost all commercial

3
00:00:09,668 --> 00:00:14,169
step2cam: an open-source experiment in automatic CAM planning

4
00:00:14,169 --> 00:00:19,707
A web demo shows each step, and lets you try your own part

5
00:00:19,707 --> 00:00:24,242
The demo and the planner are untested experiments. Feedback welcome

6
00:00:24,242 --> 00:00:28,858
First, find what has to be machined: 31 features in this plate

7
00:00:28,858 --> 00:00:36,803
The features come from specify-core, an open-source STEP library

8
00:00:36,803 --> 00:00:41,227
step2cam then plans the setups, workholding, tools and order

9
00:00:41,227 --> 00:00:47,272
Every choice names the written rule behind it

10
00:00:47,272 --> 00:00:54,735
camlib, an open-source CAM engine, turns the plan into toolpaths

11
00:00:54,735 --> 00:01:03,651
It simulates every move, cutting the stock away as it goes

12
00:01:03,651 --> 00:01:09,885
Each setup is simulated in its vise, from the stock the last one left

13
00:01:09,885 --> 00:01:15,086
and checks the moves against the stock, the tools and the vise

14
00:01:15,086 --> 00:01:20,383
Switch to a Haas VF-2: the same part, replanned with its tooling

15
00:01:20,383 --> 00:01:28,143
camlib writes the G-code, then decodes and checks it before release

16
00:01:28,143 --> 00:01:34,143
Two of the plate's Haas programs: facing the flipped plate, and the front holes

17
00:01:34,143 --> 00:01:41,248
Where camlib can't prove a program safe, it refuses to write it

18
00:01:41,248 --> 00:01:47,248
Try it with the sample parts, or your own STEP file
